{"name":"Herbal Green","hex":"#c9b23d","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"herbal","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made from or with herbs.","Made from natural herbs specifically as opposed to from synthetic materials."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A manual of herbs and their medical uses.","An herbal supplement."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/herbal"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Herbal","blocks":[{"text":"A herbal is a book containing the names and descriptions of plants, usually with information on their medicinal, tonic, culinary, toxic, hallucinatory, aromatic, or magical powers, and the legends associated with them. A herbal may also classify the plants it describes, may give recipes for herbal extracts, tinctures, or potions, and sometimes include mineral and animal medicaments in addition to those obtained from plants. Herbals were often illustrated to assist plant identification."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Dioscorides_De_Materia_Medica_Byzantium_15th_century.jpg/330px-Dioscorides_De_Materia_Medica_Byzantium_15th_century.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Herbal","wikidata":"Q6165948"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Teofrasto_Orto_botanico_detail.jpg/500px-Teofrasto_Orto_botanico_detail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Teofrasto_Orto_botanico_detail.jpg/500px-Teofrasto_Orto_botanico_detail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Statue of Theophrastus c. 371 – c. 287 BCE, Orto botanico di Palermo","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATeofrasto_Orto_botanico_detail.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8b/Ebers7766.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8b/Ebers7766.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"A page from the Ebers Papyrus, the most complete and extensive of surviving ancient herbals","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEbers7766.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Arabic_herbal_medicine_guidebook.jpeg/330px-Arabic_herbal_medicine_guidebook.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Arabic_herbal_medicine_guidebook.jpeg/330px-Arabic_herbal_medicine_guidebook.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Arabic Book of Simple Drugs (c. 1334) from Dioscorides’ De Materia Medica. British Museum","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AArabic_herbal_medicine_guidebook.jpeg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/AlbertusMagnus.jpg/250px-AlbertusMagnus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d0/AlbertusMagnus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/AlbertusMagnus.jpg/250px-AlbertusMagnus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Albertus Magnus c. 1193–1280, author of De Vegetabilibus","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAlbertusMagnus.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Elizabeth_Blackwell00.jpg/330px-Elizabeth_Blackwell00.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Elizabeth_Blackwell00.jpg/330px-Elizabeth_Blackwell00.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Illustration from Elizabeth Blackwell's A Curious Herbal (1737)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AElizabeth_Blackwell00.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Otto_Brunfels01.jpg/500px-Otto_Brunfels01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikime